// panic
// #4354, the ticket's own constraint: removing the for-of element load's bounds
// test must not change WHEN a panic fires for an out-of-range program. The
// removal is proven for exactly one access — the element the loop header just
// tested — and an ordinary `xs[j]` in the same body is a different access with
// no proof behind it.
//
// `xs[i + 1]` walks off the end on the LAST iteration and nowhere earlier, so
// the progress lines below pin the ITERATION, not just the fact of a panic. A
// bounds-check elimination that generalised from "the index is the loop's own
// induction variable" to "any index derived from it" would run one element past
// the buffer here and print `next=` followed by whatever word is adjacent —
// exit 0, plausible number, no crash. That is the failure shape this case
// exists to catch, and an exit-code-only fixture cannot see it.
//
// The progress goes to STDERR because the golden harness compares stderr for a
// `// panic` case and ignores stdout.
fn main() {
  let xs = [11, 22, 33]
  for (i, x) of xs {
    eprint("i=${i} x=${x}\n")
    eprint("next=${xs[i + 1]}\n")
  }
  eprint("unreachable\n")
}
